# Shell scripts are LF, wherever they are checked out.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true on the Windows machine releases are cut on, and with no
# rule here a clean checkout rewrites every `.sh` this repository stores with LF
# into CRLF. `packaging/publish-site.ps1` then publishes `packaging/install.sh`
# and `packaging/macos/verify-macos.sh` out of that checkout, and `sh` on a CRLF
# script fails on its first line: task-1934 ran `tools/release-verify-linux.sh`
# from a fresh worktree and got `$'\r': command not found`, then
# `set: pipefail: invalid option name`, then a syntax error.
#
# `packaging/install.sh` escaped by accident. It contains a literal carriage
# return inside a `tr -d` argument, and git leaves a file that already carries
# one alone, so the one script whose CRLF would have been noticed immediately is
# the one that was never converted. Seventeen others were.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The interop fixtures are bytes, not text (task-2036). `tests/interop/<version>/`
# holds a database one published release wrote, the log segment it left, and the
# answers it gave. The databases must never be converted, and `expected.tsv` is
# compared byte for byte between releases - so it is LF everywhere, whatever
# `core.autocrlf` says on the machine that checked it out.
/tests/interop/*/app.rdb binary
/tests/interop/*/app.rdb-wal.* binary
/tests/interop/*/expected.tsv text eol=lf

# The crash schedules are written by the campaigns that produce them, with
# `std::fs::write` and LF, and they are read as a diff - a change in one is a
# change in what the engine does under failure. With `core.autocrlf` true, git
# checks them out with CRLF, so every run of the durability tier left seven of
# them modified with an empty diff (task-2066 §4.4.9). A reviewer who has to
# skip past seven files that did not change stops reading the one that did, and
# a dirty tree is refused by both `packaging/ship.ps1` and `cargo publish`.
/tests/crash/** text eol=lf

# The fuzzing corpus is bytes (task-2066, "Done means"). `fuzz/corpus/<target>/`
# holds the inputs libfuzzer kept because they reached new coverage, and each
# one is whatever byte string reached it - SQL text, a page image, a segment
# header, a run of bytes that is none of those. Left as text, `core.autocrlf`
# on the Windows machine releases are cut on rewrites every `0x0a` in them to
# `0x0d 0x0a` on checkout, and the corpus stops being the input that found the
# coverage. It would not fail visibly: the fuzzer would run happily on the
# rewritten bytes and reach less than it did.
fuzz/corpus/** -text
